# Side-chain control of porosity closure in single- and multiple-peptide-based porous materials by cooperative folding

## abstract
Porous materials are attractive for separation and catalysis-these applications rely on selective interactions between host materials and guests. In metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), these interactions can be controlled through a flexible structural response to the presence of guests. Here we report a MOF that consists of glycyl-serine dipeptides coordinated to metal centres, and has a structure that evolves from a solvated porous state to a desolvated non-porous state as a result of ordered cooperative, displacive and conformational changes of the peptide. This behaviour is driven by hydrogen bonding that involves the side-chain hydroxyl groups of the serine. A similar cooperative closure (reminiscent of the folding of proteins) is also displayed with multipeptide solid solutions. For these, the combination of different sequences of amino acids controls the framework's response to the presence of guests in a nonlinear way. This functional control can be compared to the effect of single-point mutations in proteins, in which exchange of single amino acids can radically alter structure and function.

Porous materials are attractive for separation and catalysis—these applications rely on selective interactions between host materials and guests. In metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), these interactions can be controlled through a flexible structural response to the presence of guests. Here we report a MOF that consists of glycyl–serine dipeptides coordinated to metal centres, and has a structure that evolves from a solvated porous state to a desolvated non-porous state as a result of ordered cooperative, displacive and conformational changes of the peptide. This behaviour is driven by hydrogen bonding that involves the side-chain hydroxyl groups of the serine. A similar cooperative closure (reminiscent of the folding of proteins) is also displayed with multipeptide solid solutions. For these, the combination of different sequences of amino acids controls the framework’s response to the presence of guests in a nonlinear way. This functional control can be compared to the effect of single-point mutations in proteins, in which exchange of single amino acids can radically alter structure and function.
M etal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of crystalline, microporous materials built from the combination of metal ions or clusters with organic linkers1. Their intrinsic porosity and the presence of metal sites confer interesting properties on these solids that are of use in gas storage and separation2,3, heterogeneous catalysis4,5, drug delivery6, sensing7,8, and a range of other applications. Furthermore, their rich structural diversity, generally controlled by the topology and denticity of the organic synthon, enables access to pores of variable size9,10 or fine-tuning of their physical behaviour by the introduction of functional organic sites that decorate the pore walls and tune the guest-to-framework interaction11. Thus, these systems offer chemically controllable ordered porosity because of the functional role of the organic linker in determining the internal surface chemistry and the structural response to guests.
Biologically derived linkers such as amino acids12 or nucleobases13 have proved successful in the synthesis of $\mathrm{MOFs^{14}}$ . These molecules afford extensive chemical functionality, including chirality and versatile metal-binding modes. Peptides are a particularly diverse set of candidate linkers within this class because of the array of side-chain chemistries and the role of the sequence of amino-acid residue in controlling their behaviour.
Peptides differ from the classical rigid aromatic-based MOF linkers in the extensive conformational space that is accessible through low-energy torsions. Such modes enable protein folding, which, in response to environmental changes, can take place in an ordered manner between well-defined structures that involve large atomic displacements15. The torsional manifold of the peptide chains, controlled by the nature and sequence of the amino-acid residues, allows the required conformations to be accessed, and thereby enables key biochemical processes. As the torsional response of an oligopeptide chain, although less complex than that of a protein, is also controlled by the amino-acid sequence, there is an opportunity to use these motions to control the guest response of a MOF.
Recently, it was shown that the disordered torsional response of Gly-Ala (GA) in $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{GA})_{2}$ closes the pore space on guest removal16. This contrasts with the classical rigid behaviour of the peptide-based framework $\operatorname{Zn}(\mathrm{Gly-Thr})_{2}$ $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GT})_{2})^{17}$ , a robust permanently porous material with a structure that is independent of the presence or nature of guests. Unlike GA, GT is conformationally invariant in $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GT})_{2}$ , as it forms a chelate with ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and also establishes additional intraframework hydrogen bonds mediated by the $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups in the Thr side-chain, which are inaccessible to the $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ of Ala. These examples demonstrate the critical role of the side chain in controlling how the torsional degrees of freedom can be deployed in response to environmental changes.
It remains a challenge to exploit the torsional space accessible to the peptide linker within an open framework so as to induce an ordered and controlled structural response to guests that is more directly analogous to the changes undergone by proteins. We show that changing one dipeptide residue, and using Gly-Ser (GS) as the linker, allows ordered torsional change in the resulting framework and displacement on guest loss to effect reversible closure of the pores. This torsional change is controlled by the side-chain hydrogen bonding in $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GS})_{2}$ and coupled with large ordered displacements of the peptide linkers to suppress reversibly $87\%$ of the initial pore volume. The Ser side-chain retains the $-\mathrm{OH}$ functionality of Thr, but the change from a secondary to a primary alcohol is sufficient to produce this dramatic change in guest response. The construction of multiple GlyX dipeptide frameworks from the three X residues (Ala, Thr, Ser), the side chains of which each impose distinct functions, is then demonstrated to control the overall cooperative MOF response in a tuneable way through peptide torsions and side-chain chemistry, attaining responses that are not linear combinations of those of the end-member single-peptide materials.
## Results and discussion
Synthesis and structure of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}(\mathbf{G}\mathbf{S})_{2}$ and multiple-peptide MOFs. Solvothermal reaction of zinc(II) nitrate with glycyl–serine in a methanolic solution slightly basified with 1 M NaOH(aq) yields prismatic colourless crystals of $\mathrm{Zn(GS)}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ which crystallize in the chiral monoclinic space group $P2_{1}2_{1}2$ (Flack parameter $=0.00(2),$ ). $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GS})_{2}$ is isostructural to $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{GA})_{2})$ .(solvent)16, and can be described as a chemically modified analogue that incorporates hydroxyl $\left(-\mathrm{OH}\right)$ groups in the $\upalpha$ -carbon position of the C-terminus residue of the oligopeptide. The primary $-\mathrm{OH}$ side chain thus imposes a different structure than that found for the secondary $-\mathrm{OH}$ of the Thr residue in $\mathrm{Zn(GT)}_{2}$ (ref. 17). $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GS})_{2}$ is a two-dimensional (2D) layered MOF built from the stacking of neutral $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{GS})_{2}]$ square-like sheets. The sheets are formed by tetrahedral $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions that are linked to four peptides acting as $\upmu_{2}$ -bridges.
The channels are oriented along [001] and give a solvent-accessible volume of $27.7\%$ ( $267.6\mathrm{\AA}^{3}$ at $100~\mathrm{K}$ per unit cell (Fig. 1d and
Side-chain-enabled ordered closure of the framework. The effect of the hydrogen bonding from the Ser side-chain on the behaviour of the $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GS})_{2}$ structure is shown by the dramatic structural change in isostructural $\mathrm{Zn}[(\mathrm{GS})_{0.75}(\mathrm{GT})_{0.25}]_{2}$ on solvent removal. Removal of guests from isostructural $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{GA})_{2}$ produced extensive local torsional disorder of the GlyAla dipeptide, as shown by solid-state $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ cross-polarization/magic angle spinning NMR (CP/MAS NMR) spectroscopy16. CP/MAS NMR spectroscopy shows that desolvation of $\mathrm{Zn}[\mathrm{(GS)}_{0.75}(\mathrm{GT})_{0.25}]_{2}$ affords a material that is well-ordered at a local level (Fig. 3a). The solvated solid displays equivalent environments for the GS/GT amino acids at 179.3, 171.1 and $44.3\mathrm{ppm}$ , which can be assigned to carboxylate, carbonyl and the $-\mathrm{CH}_{2^{-}}$ group adjacent to the amine. Resonances in a 3:1 ratio at 65.4, 55.1, 68.8 and $57.9\ \mathrm{ppm}$ are attributed to the inequivalent $\mathrm{-CH}_{2}\mathrm{-}$ and $-\mathrm{CH}-$ groups in the GS and GT amino acids, respectively, consistent with the GS:GT 75:25 ratio. The presence of MeOH is confirmed by the resonance at $50\mathrm{ppm}$ . The remaining peak at $19.2\mathrm{ppm}$ is assigned to $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ from the GT side-chain. The MeOH resonance is no longer present in the desolvated state, which confirms the successful removal of solvent. New resonances at 48.2 and $22.4~\mathrm{ppm}$ can be attributed to $-\mathrm{CH}_{2^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ environments in different orientation and/or bonding modes to those present in the solvated state. Figure $^{3\mathrm{b},\mathrm{c}}$ displays the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ CP/MAS spectra and corresponding PXRD patterns that monitor the progressive changes coupled to desolvation. On solvent removal, the MeOH resonance decreases, and resonances attributed to the desolvated configuration increase. These intermediate configurations are a mixture of locally ordered solvated and desolvated states. This is in good agreement with the evolution of the PXRD patterns on guest removal, with the guest-free state retaining similar scattering power, and thus crystallinity, to the as-grown material, consistent with the order seen locally; this demonstrates the homogeneous bulk nature of the desolvation process.
This specific conformational change on guest loss is intrinsic to the introduction of Ser into the dipeptide sequence, as it is driven by the energetically favourable formation of intralayer hydrogen bonds between the side-chain $-\mathrm{OH}$ and the terminal $-\mathrm{COO}^{-}$ groups of C-terminal residues from peptides on opposite faces of the channel wall in the closed state (O11...H9, 1.9622(18) Å). Energetically, this counterbalances the breaking of hydrogen bonds between the framework and the guest in the open configuration. The combination of torsion and displacement is an ordered variant of the disordered closure mechanism proposed for $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{GA})_{2}$ , driven by the specific directional hydrogen bonding afforded by the Ser side-chain and the need to engage these groups in hydrogen bonding. This affords a mechanically stable long-range ordered closed state that gives single-crystal diffraction, which enables the structure of the closed state to be determined unambiguously. Refinement of the PXRD pattern of desolvated $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GS})_{2}$ confirms that it is isostructural to the closed state of $\mathrm{Zn}[(\mathrm{GS})_{0.75}(\mathrm{GT})_{0.25}]_{2}$ and so the structure of the single-peptide phase follows an equivalent structural evolution from porous/open to non-porous/closed as a result of dynamical ordered closure on desolvation.
All of the solids that adopt the $\mathrm{Zn}{\left(\mathrm{GS}/\mathrm{GA}\right)}_{2}$ structure undergo dynamical ordered closure on guest loss. Even $10\%$ GS substitution into $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{GA})_{2}$ is sufficient to drive ordered closure by nucleating the cooperative torsional change required through hydrogen bonding. Within this flexible (F) class there are two categories of behaviour. Structures that adopt this topology, in which all the dipeptides have hydrogen-bonding side chains (GS and GS:GT for $\mathrm{GS}\geq50\%,$ , remain closed to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ up to 1 bar at $195\mathrm{K}$ and up to the highest measured pressure of 15 bar at 273 and $298\mathrm{K},$ , in contrast to $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{GA})_{2}$ , which admits $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ above a gate pressure of 0.2 bar at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ and 2 bar at $273\mathrm{K}$ . The interaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules with $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GS})_{2}$ (and the isostructural all-OH side-chain materials) is not strong enough to break the side chain to carboxylate hydrogen bonds that stabilize the closed state and trigger the reopening of the pores up to 15 bar at $273\ \mathrm{K}.$ However, exposure to the polar guest MeOH at room temperature results in uptake to re-form the open structure driven by the formation of hydrogen bonds between the side chain and guest (Fig. 2c). These materials are classified as flexible non-porous $\mathrm{(F_{npCO}}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ ) in recognition of their closure to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over the studied conditions.
## Conclusion and outlook
The torsional degrees of freedom of the GS dipeptide reposition the OH-bearing side chain of the Ser residue to enable a large translational displacement of the peptide. These coupled motions form a hydrogen bond that closes the structure of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GS})_{2}$ on guest loss in an ordered manner, driven by this conformational change to afford a translationally symmetric closed state in which the loss of hydrogen bonding to the guest is compensated by the new host–host interaction. This contrasts with the disordered closure of $\operatorname{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})_{2}$ , in which the absence of directional bonding associated with the methyl side chain of Ala offers no unique closure path. The strength of the resulting hydrogen bond makes the structure openable only by polar guests, in contrast to the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -accessible porosity offered by the isostructural $\operatorname{Zn}(\operatorname{GA})_{2}$ and the rigid $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GT})_{2}$ . This demonstrates the changes in function associated with a difference in one aminoacid residue that results from coupling of side-chain functionality to torsional changes.
Despite these large displacements, crystallinity is retained because of the ordered nature of the closure favoured by the directional nature of the hydrogen bonds that drive it. In proteins, coupled torsional and translational displacements are associated with folding into conformations appropriate for a given environment and function41,42, and single-point mutations can alter structure and function radically. For example, the Drosophila GABA receptor displays insecticide resistance when Ala is exchanged with Ser and this is reduced by Thr to Ala mutation43,44. The sidechain control of the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Zn}}(\mathrm{XY})_{2}$ dipeptide framework function permits the tuning of their properties over a wide range through the formation of multipeptide solid solutions that display nonlinear property variations because of synergic interactions between the functional groups on the constituent peptides, which sets the challenge to order the assembly of these multiple peptides within solids to access new properties.
